#edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459168 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186458834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459168 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bec51248-9b9b-569c-a543-cda111929d9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141485" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459233 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $T$ is not a bijection, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n\nIII. $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4841bd4c-e53a-5f1a-ae44-1f39564910ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459233 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ In turn, there is $v\\ne 0$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459244 #edb/ref 1071 "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6acf0a48-db0b-58e8-8817-08a155161bed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459244 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459245 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459245 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459245 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\nI. $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n\nII. $T$ is a bijection\n\nIII. $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459245 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6afa206b-a720-5d99-9269-3c50ce4125f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459245 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ which means  $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141446" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459267 #edb/ref 1071 "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9d1798c-418f-589b-8399-947e298c1f4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459267 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186459134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459301 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459301 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459301 #edb/ref 1071 "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f070189a-9c50-508c-9a92-9de0a01ed5ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186459301 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expression are $\\{0\\}$ and $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
